{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/105427-what-did-the-guilds-do","id":105427,"title":"What did the Guilds do?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-04-04T00:35:47-07:00","id":105427,"title":"What did the Guilds do?","content":"This is the newbiest of newbie questions, so apologies in advance: what were the roles of the Guilds during the time of the Nameless Empire?<br /> <br /> I've read and re-read the core book over and over again, but the Guilds always confuse the hell out of me. The Su-Menent were death priests, or maybe regular priests, the Sesha-Hebsu were scribes, and I guess the Tef-Aabhi were engineers, but I could not possibly tell you what the Mesen-Nebu or Maa-Kep did during the time among the living. The Maa-Kep especially confuse me, but clarification on all five of our friends (six, I suppose) would be most welcome, as I know I'm not grasping them well and certainly couldn't describe them to someone else.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T00:39:53-07:00","id":105430,"title":"","content":"The Maa-Kep were overseers and slave-drivers. The Mesen-Nebu were alchemists and metallurgists and the like.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T00:42:57-07:00","id":105433,"title":"","content":"Actually it seems Mummy from all guilds could have been basic Slaves, security, or laborers associated with the guilds rather then actual priests and alchemists themselves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T09:04:42-07:00","id":105600,"title":"","content":"Maa-Keep were the slave leaders of the people. They were the ones marching around whipping people for the overseers. They were basically middle management. Now, they're secret police. Work together... for our glorious leader's goals. Or else. Boot lickers who haven't put down their whips.<br /> <br /> Sesha-Hebsu were scribes and glorified librarians; they served as historians and keepers of the law books. Even taxes. Anything that needed to be written down, they did. Mostly, they're historians now who like writing history the way they want it to be known. Corruption is a vibe in every guild, though, don't forget.<br /> <br /> Mesen-Nebu, though they are called alchemists, actually tended to serve as a merchant / craftsman guild; potentially prostitution as well. They were all about the value of precious gems, metals, and apparently beauty made its way into that mix as well. Modern day alchemists control businesses instead of craftsman guilds.<br /> <br /> Su-Menent were funeral priests. Necromancer-zombie-raiser-preists, just as much. Probably closest thing to a direct assistant to the Sorcerer part of the Sorcerer-Kings we got. They're also the religious leaders on top of sorcerers.<br /> <br /> Tef-Aabhi were the architects, engineers, and builders, controlling the world through geomancy.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> No real information about the Deceived as a guild. Something to do with &quot;Will&quot; and &quot;Name-magic.&quot; Potentially a link to Apotheosis as a group. <br /> <br /> Shuankhsen were slaves, prisoners, and outcasts.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 04-04-2014, 12:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T11:09:46-07:00","id":105662,"title":"","content":"Some of the guilds can be stretched pretty far. For example, the maa-kep served as educated assistants to other guilds. Its probable that maa-kep also served as area's not-so-secret police. Mesen-nebu could serve as any sort of scientist, a mine supervisor, inventor. Seshu-hebsu, as someone mentioned, could be anyone related to bureaucracy or paperwork, as well as legislature and government service. Tef-aabhi could've been civil engineers, social planners, mathematicians, and geneticists (of a primitive sort). Su-menent could be templar, morticians, clergy, &quot;sorcerers&quot;, philosophers, and physicians. Of course, the way all of these jobs are styled wouldn't be like the modern ones. I used modern terms out of convenience. But Irem had some primal equivalent of those things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T11:51:14-07:00","id":105703,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eldagusto</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/105427-what-did-the-guilds-do?p=105433#post105433\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually it seems Mummy from all guilds could have been basic Slaves, security, or laborers associated with the guilds rather then actual priests and alchemists themselves.</div> </div> </div> </div>The book's pretty clear on the Arisen having been drawn from the Iremite equivalent of the middle class/middle-management. There's a fairly wide gulf between &quot;no Arisen who were Iremite rulers&quot; and &quot;no Arisen who weren't drawn from the lowest rung of society&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T13:09:56-07:00","id":105776,"title":"","content":"Well, I also suggest checking out tvtropes.org. They're pretty good at giving strong ideas about what these games are.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 04-04-2014, 04:13 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-04T15:54:41-07:00","id":105884,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cleverest of Things</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/105427-what-did-the-guilds-do?p=105662#post105662\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, the way all of these jobs are styled wouldn't be like the modern ones. I used modern terms out of convenience. But Irem had some primal equivalent of those things.</div> </div> </div> </div>More particularly, you'll find that as you go back further into history, &quot;jobs&quot; tend to be less specialized. A priest in ancient egypt was probably <i>also</i> a doctor, mortician, sorcerer, and philosopher. It was less &quot;you could be any of those things&quot; and more &quot;you are probably <i>all</i> of those things, but you might have one or two that you're especially better at&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","name":"Gabby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T06:35:24-07:00","id":107408,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gabby</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/105427-what-did-the-guilds-do?p=105884#post105884\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> More particularly, you'll find that as you go back further into history, &quot;jobs&quot; tend to be less specialized. A priest in ancient egypt was probably <i>also</i> a doctor, mortician, sorcerer, and philosopher. It was less &quot;you could be any of those things&quot; and more &quot;you are probably <i>all</i> of those things, but you might have one or two that you're especially better at&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Smaller communities, more dificult travel, more limited knowledge and technique.<br /> <br /> A doctor in an Iremite village would probably learn most of the actual skills and knowledge of medicine in pretty short order and then only meet other doctors with new ideas and technique once every now and then if they travelled to a different village or went to an urban center for trade and brought some new knowledge back. They'd fill th erest of their time doing other stuff - animal husbandry, funeral rites/body preparation/etc.<br /> <br /> So yeah, I could see 'polymath' professions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/291-scutarii","name":"Scutarii","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar291_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"702"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T09:38:08-07:00","id":107496,"title":"","content":"My impression was that the Guilds were less strictly professions and more organisations based around completing a central task. So to give an example, the Alchemists start as the group whose task is to provide the tools for Irem and therefore their membership would be made up of people in the relevant professions: slave-drivers to control the mining of materials, smiths to actually make things and presumably the logistics person who connects the two. They're all Born of Gold, but their actual jobs and skills are quite different.<br /> <br /> On top of that the Guilds gradually build up their own mystery religions around their tasks to the point that they start doing things that aren't really related to their original task any more. So asking what the Guilds 'do' begins to loose it's coherence.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T10:35:25-07:00","id":107536,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/105427-what-did-the-guilds-do?p=107496#post107496\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My impression was that the Guilds were less strictly professions and more organisations based around completing a central task. So to give an example, the Alchemists start as the group whose task is to provide the tools for Irem and therefore their membership would be made up of people in the relevant professions: slave-drivers to control the mining of materials, smiths to actually make things and presumably the logistics person who connects the two. They're all Born of Gold, but their actual jobs and skills are quite different.<br /> <br /> On top of that the Guilds gradually build up their own mystery religions around their tasks to the point that they start doing things that aren't really related to their original task any more. So asking what the Guilds 'do' begins to loose it's coherence.</div> </div> </div> </div>That is my impression. After all for instance you could have a member of the Scribe Guild who was illiterate, but was message deliverer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T10:47:18-07:00","id":107545,"title":"","content":"Especially now, this is true. In the millenia since mortal life, the Guilds put less importance on the actual profession the Mummy had in irem, and more a wider philosophy, mystery religion, and task at hand. The details, such as what EXACTLY a given mummy did for a living in Irem, are largely unimportant to the arisen compared to keeping their Memory cohesive and giving their eternity some general purpose.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T11:04:41-07:00","id":107554,"title":"","content":"My theory as to the Guild's metaphysical significance has more to do with having a whole Sothic Turn in the Duat to be trained/built, rather then their experiences in their first lifetime. After all many of the greatest magic savvy sorcerers/alchemists/necromancers/mystics were never given the Rite of Return. It seemed they choose people based on loyalty and perceived potential. Just think about it, I highly doubt the Arisen were Utterance Wielding mystics in life. Most of the NPCs mention Guilds having Alchemists and Magicians beyond the Shan'iatu but the NPCs themselves were never them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T11:14:09-07:00","id":107559,"title":"","content":"Makes sense. After all, the Guildhalls book suggests a theory that mortal sekhem sorcery is related to ren-heka.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T11:19:42-07:00","id":107562,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cleverest of Things</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/105427-what-did-the-guilds-do?p=107559#post107559\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Makes sense. After all, the Guildhalls book suggests a theory that mortal sekhem sorcery is related to ren-heka.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Really?! That's cool! That's type of stuff I heard people ask about and nobody mentioned anything about it till now!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T11:20:56-07:00","id":107565,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/105427-what-did-the-guilds-do?p=105600#post105600\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Tef-Aabhi were the architects, engineers, and builders, controlling the world through geomancy.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> No real information about the Deceived as a guild. Something to do with &quot;Will&quot; and &quot;Name-magic.&quot; Potentially a link to Apotheosis as a group.<br /> <br /> Shuankhsen were slaves, prisoners, and outcasts.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually we know they were the Artist Guild. Something about Art resonates with the True Name of a thing. Very Supernal philosophy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T14:01:50-07:00","id":107672,"title":"","content":"It is mentioned somewhere in the Seshu-Hebsu laws. Its the in game canonical reason why we haven't seen stats for Sorcerers of sekhem yet--they're outlawed, and trafficking with them is as bad to conservative Scribes as Shuanksen. IIRC, its a death-worthy offense, because they consider the sorcery to be the Shan-Iatu's domain solely. It might be in the text or a sidebar, but they mention something about a theory that mortal sorcery could be connected to the magics of the lost guild. It was burried deep--I'd reread the maa'kep, tef'aabhi, talismans, and storytellers sections two or three times before i dug through the whole section and found that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-07T14:28:01-07:00","id":107706,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> My theory as to the Guild's metaphysical significance has more to do with having a whole Sothic Turn in the Duat to be trained/built, rather then their experiences in their first lifetime. </div> </div> </div>Guildhalls seems to be imply that to at least some degree the guildhalls have gained their significance through being invoked by the Arisen over 5 thousand years. Kinda like how the mythologies of Krewes in Sin-Eater gain power by being invoked by larger groups. I believe it's the part which suggests that, hypothetically, a Mummy could carve out their own Guild.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I highly doubt the Arisen were Utterance Wielding mystics in life. </div> </div> </div>Is it not outright stated that Utterances are an unintentional result of being Arisen?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Very Supernal philosophy. </div> </div> </div>Glad to see I'm not the only one who came away with that impression.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-08T07:10:58-07:00","id":108177,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Scutarii</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/105427-what-did-the-guilds-do?p=107408#post107408\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Smaller communities, more dificult travel, more limited knowledge and technique.<br /> <br /> A doctor in an Iremite village would probably learn most of the actual skills and knowledge of medicine in pretty short order and then only meet other doctors with new ideas and technique once every now and then if they travelled to a different village or went to an urban center for trade and brought some new knowledge back. They'd fill th erest of their time doing other stuff - animal husbandry, funeral rites/body preparation/etc.<br /> <br /> So yeah, I could see 'polymath' professions.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean, egypt (and if we have to look at a real world place to determine what Irem was <i>mostly</i> like, that's the place) had cities aplenty, it's not like we're necessarily talking about 100-person villages. It's more that, for one thing, there was a limited amount to know about each of these things. They were startlingly advanced in medicine, but it still wouldn't take nearly as long to learn everything anyone knew about medicine and anatomy as it does now: there just wasn't as much accumulated knowledge. Likewise, medicine was considered a subset of magic (because the egyptian view of magic was weird. It was basically &quot;the sum total of stuff that educated people can do to affect the world&quot;), so if you were a doctor you were also a magician in a lot of other senses. And considering the place you learned to be a magician was probably a temple, you were probably also a priest. And since you already knew anatomy <i>and</i> the proper religious rituals, there's a pretty strong chance (depending on whose clergy you were in) you were involved in embalming.<br /> <br /> Really, it was less that the communities were smaller (they were, but they were still pretty big) and more that education was more concentrated, both for normal feudal knowledge=power reasons and also because of a belief that literacy and writing were literally magic and you couldn't just trust any random asshole with it. If you were educated much at all, it's because you were part of a very small priest, scribe, or artisan cadre within the larger community that placed you somewhere between the nobility/royalty and the peasants (misguidedly called slaves by a bunch of historians, but probably <i>actually</i> slaves in Irem because it seems like Egypt was basically the survivors of Irem going &quot;okay, what if we tried civilization again, but <i>weren't</i> complete dicks at every opportunity?&quot;). And really tiny educated communities tend to end up with a bunch of people wearing a lot of different hats.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":996,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","user":"Gabby","date":". 04-08-2014, 10:26 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/996-gabby","name":"Gabby","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"226"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}